  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K.P.Latheef vs The Food Inspector on 15 March, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 15Th Day Of March 2016/25Th Phalguna, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 320 of 2011 ( ) -------------------------------- Crl.A 178/2008 of Iii Addl. Sessions Court, Kozhikode Cc 457/2003 of J.M.F.C.-V, Kozhikode Revision Petitioners/Appellants/Accused: --------------------------------------- 1. K.P.Latheef, Aged 42 Years, S/O.Marakkar, M/S.Calicut Sweets, Room No.17 I.G.Bus Stand Building, I.G.Road, Calicut Residing At Poyil Parambil, Arakkinar P.O. Calicut-673028 2. Chokly Muhammed Kutty, Aged 61 Years, S/O.Kammunni, M/S.Calicut Sweets Room No-17, I.G.Bus Stand Building, I.G.Road Calicut, R/A. Meladippadam House, Nallalam P.O. Calicut-673027 By Advs.Sri.V.G.Arun Sri.T.R.Harikumar Respondents/Respondents/Complainant & State: -------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector Kozhikode Corporation, Kozhikode -673020 2. State Of Kerala, K.P.Latheef vs The Food Inspector on 15 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145859343/ 1 Rep.By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam, Kochi-11 R By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 15-03-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P.No. 320 of 2011 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 15th day of March, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 5.07.1999, PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 600 gram of banana chips from the first accused. The second accused was the licensee of the shop and the third accused was the manufacturer. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The public analyst submitted Ext.P10 report stating that the sample contained synthetic food colour tartrazine in violation of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts.P1 to P16 were marked for the prosecution. Exts.D1 to D3 were marked for the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P10 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. K.P.Latheef vs The Food Inspector on 15 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145859343/ 2 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P10 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. K.P.Latheef vs The Food Inspector on 15 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145859343/ 2 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
